KPL/SCLK

Hayabusa2 SCLK file
-------------------
The ``Time Calibration System'' is commonly used in JAXA/ISAS after
Hisaki satellite also known as SPRINT-A and EXCEED. It generates time
calibration table for each spacecraft/satellite.
This calibration table is converted into SPICE SCLK format by
``Level 1 Time Series Data for SPICE (L1TSD SPICE).''

L1TSD SPICE software version: 2.06 (Release: 20190329p)

  START DATE (UTC):  2014-12-02T21:20:02.551467
  STOP  DATE (UTC):  2023-02-14T01:34:36.268951

The original filename is hyb2_20230214_v01.tsc .


Partition numbers and Spacecraft Clock Reset
--------------------------------------------
The spacecraft clock is a 32-bit counter. One bit represents about
1/32 seconds and it cycles through 0 approximately every 4.25 years.

The Hayabusa2 spacecraft was launched on December 3, 2014. Therefore,
the first reset could be calculated on March 5, 2019. However, the
reset was manually performed before the asteroid proximity phase by
the Hayabusa2 Project Team on

  2017-09-05T05:00:00 UTC (Later detail analysis: 04:59:58).

The second manual reset was performed on

  2021-05-17T21:59:59.414585 UTC .

Partition numbers can be incremented when the two consecutive time
correlation packets don't satisfy a continuous condition for an
interpolation which is automatically calculated by the Time
Calibration System. The list of clock related events including
discontinuity is summarized below:

  No.   Approximate date of event     Event
  ---   -------------------------     ----------------------
    1   2014-12-02T21:20:02 UTC       Just before the launch
    2   2017-09-05T04:59:59 UTC       Manual reset
    3   2021-05-17T21:59:59 UTC       Manual reset
    4   2022-02-20T11:06:11 UTC       Discontinuity
    5   2023-02-12T13:03:01 UTC       Discontinuity



